mc(2,2,"Case","A child plays at being a doctor, a teacher and a parent one at a time, copying people close to them. Which stage of Mead's theory is this?","Play stage",["Game stage","Preparatory stage","Resocialisation stage"],"In the play stage the child acts out the roles of significant others one at a time.");
mc(3,2,"Case","A teenager in a football team understands what every teammate must do and follows the team's rules at the same time. In Mead's theory this is the…","Game stage, with the generalised other",["Play stage, with significant others","Preparatory stage, with significant others","Game stage, with the looking-glass self"],"In the game stage the child grasps many roles at once and the rules of the generalised other.");
mc(2,2,"Case","A student checks how many likes a post gets to decide whether they are “funny” or “boring”. This illustrates…","The looking-glass self",["Cognitive dissonance","Role conflict","Resocialisation"],"Cooley (1902): our self-image is based on how we think others see us.");
mc(3,2,"Case","A manager who is also a parent must stay late for a crucial meeting on the evening of their child's recital. This is an example of…","Role conflict",["Cognitive dissonance","Role reinforcement","Resocialisation"],"Role conflict = contradictory expectations from the multiplicity of roles we play.");
mc(3,2,"Case","A smoker who knows tobacco is harmful says “it's not such a big deal”. According to Festinger, they are…","Trivialising their dissonance",["Experiencing role conflict","Taking the role of the other","Going through resocialisation"],"Cognitive dissonance can be reduced by trivialising: “it's not such a big deal”.");
mc(2,2,"Case","A recruit enters military boot camp: hair cut, uniform, new rules, cut off from civilian life. Which concepts apply?","Total institution and resocialisation",["Primary socialisation and the play stage","Conspicuous consumption and status","Social bond theory and labelling"],"Total institutions (prison, military, mental institution) replace earlier socialisations: resocialisation.");
mc(3,2,"Harlow","What did Harlow's “cloth mother” vs “wire mother” experiment show?","That affectional contact matters more than being fed",["That monkeys prefer the surrogate that feeds them","That isolation has no lasting effect","That social learning only occurs through food"],"The monkeys clung to the soft cloth surrogate; those with only the feeding wire mother survived but were socially dysfunctional.");
mc(2,2,"Veblen","A wealthy person buys a very expensive, brand-new gadget mainly to show their status. Veblen would call this…","Conspicuous consumption",["Looking-glass self","Cultural lag","Role reinforcement"],"Veblen (1899): high-status groups flaunt novel goods to signal status, and lower groups emulate them.");
mc(3,2,"Bronfenbrenner","Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model says that a person's development is shaped by…","Nested environmental systems",["A single dominant agent of socialisation","Genetic traits only","The individual's rational choices alone"],"Development is influenced by several nested environments (family, school, wider society…).");
tf(1,2,"Primary socialisation relies on “significant others”.",true,"Family and close carers in early life.");
tf(2,2,"For Mead, the generalised other is the child's awareness of the demands of society as a whole.",true,"It appears in the game stage.");
tf(3,2,"Harlow's monkeys that had only the wire mother grew up socially well adjusted.",false,"They survived (they were fed) but were socially dysfunctional.");
ty(2,2,"Cooley's concept: self-image based on how we think others see us",["looking-glass self","looking glass self"],"Introduced in 1902.");
ty(3,2,"Sociologist who theorised cognitive dissonance",["Festinger","Leon Festinger"],"Discomfort from holding two conflicting cognitions.");
