mc(2,3,"Case","A government decides that mobile phones must be handed in at school gates, with detention for offenders. Which type of social control is this?","Coercion (negative sanctions)",["Persuasion (rewarding)","Cultural hegemony","Differential reinforcement"],"Coercion mechanisms use negative sanctions; persuasion mechanisms convince and reward.");
mc(3,3,"Case","A teenager steals because they cannot afford the clothes that advertising says everyone must have, and have no legitimate way to earn the money. Which theory best explains this?","Strain theory",["Social bond theory","Labelling theory","Rational choice theory"],"Strain: deviance arises when culturally approved goals cannot be reached through approved means.");
mc(3,3,"Case","A teenager is caught once for graffiti, is treated as a “delinquent” by teachers and police, and gradually starts to behave as one. Which theory is illustrated?","Labelling theory",["Strain theory","Differential reinforcement theory","Rational choice theory"],"Control agencies designate deviants, who then accept the label and act accordingly.");
mc(3,3,"Case","A person cheats on taxes after calculating that the chance of being caught is tiny compared with the gain. This is the logic of…","Rational choice theory",["Social bond theory","Strain theory","Labelling theory"],"Deviance happens when the person judges that the benefits outweigh the risks.");
mc(3,3,"Case","A young man drifts into crime after leaving school, family and his sports club. Which theory emphasises this weakening of ties?","Social bond theory",["Rational choice theory","Cultural hegemony","Labelling theory"],"Deviance becomes more likely when ties to society are weakened or broken.");
mc(3,3,"Foucault","In Foucault's analysis, what makes the panopticon an efficient tool of control?","Inmates never know when they are watched, so they police themselves",["Guards physically beat anyone who disobeys","Inmates are given rewards for denouncing others","Prisoners are isolated from each other and from guards"],"The design makes inmates feel permanently watched, giving officials knowledge inmates lack.");
mc(2,3,"Dark patterns","An app buries the “cancel subscription” button under five menus. Which dark pattern is this?","Obstruction",["Nagging","Sneaking","Confirmshaming"],"Obstruction: an unwanted path is deliberately made hard.");
mc(2,3,"Dark patterns","A cookie banner shows a bright “Accept all” button and a greyed-out “Reject”. Which dark pattern is this?","Interface interference",["Forced action","Sneaking","Disguised ads"],"The layout visually privileges one option.");
mc(3,3,"Dark patterns","A hotel booking site adds a “service fee” only on the last page, just before payment. Which dark pattern is this?","Sneaking",["Scarcity and urgency","Nagging","Obstruction"],"Sneaking: key information is hidden or delayed until you are committed.");
mc(3,3,"Dark patterns","The decline button reads “No thanks, I prefer paying full price”. Which dark pattern is this?","Confirmshaming",["Nagging","Forced action","Interface interference"],"Confirmshaming words the decline option to make you feel guilty.");
mc(3,3,"Dark patterns","A shop displays “Only 2 left! Offer ends in 03:59” with a timer that restarts on every refresh. This is…","Scarcity and urgency",["Sneaking","Disguised ads","Forced action"],"Fake countdowns or stock messages exploit fear of missing out.");
mc(3,3,"Dark patterns","Which question is the “dark-pattern test”?","Would the user make the same choice if they fully understood what was happening?",["Does the interface use bright colours?","Is the service free to the user?","Would the company lose money if the user left?"],"If the answer is no, the design is manipulative.");
mc(3,3,"Deviance","According to the functionalist view, which is NOT a function of deviance?","It eliminates the need for social rules",["It clarifies the rules","It unites a group against rule-breakers","It can promote social change"],"Functionalism sees deviance as clarifying rules, uniting the group and promoting change; it does not remove the need for rules.");
tf(2,3,"The conflict view of deviance holds that laws mainly reflect the interests of the powerful.",true,"Powerful groups label threats to their interests as deviant.");
tf(3,3,"In Durkheim's view, deviance is rooted in rapid social change and a lack of social integration.",true,"This is the origin of his idea of anomie.");
tf(2,3,"Cultural hegemony means convincing people that the status quo is right and has to be that way.",true,"A form of control by persuasion.");
ty(2,3,"Foucault's prison design in which inmates feel permanently watched",["panopticon"],"Foucault: power, knowledge and social control are intertwined.");
ty(3,3,"Dark pattern: repeated requests that keep coming back, like “Rate this app?”",["nagging"],"Nagging = repeated interruptions.");
