mc(2,4,"Case","In a society where one's occupation and rank are fixed at birth and cannot be changed, we have…","A caste system",["A meritocracy","An open class system","A conflict of status groups"],"Caste: closed stratification, no social mobility, status ascribed at birth.");
mc(3,4,"Weber","A bankrupt aristocrat has little wealth but enjoys great respect in their town. Which Weberian dimension does this illustrate?","Status, distinct from class",["Class only","Power only","Social closure"],"Status concerns esteem granted by others, which can differ from wealth (class).");
mc(3,4,"Bourdieu","A student whose parents are professors speaks the “right” way and feels at ease with museums. This is mainly…","Embodied cultural capital",["Objectified cultural capital","Institutionalised cultural capital","Economic capital"],"Embodied = how you speak, think and behave; objectified = cultural items; institutionalised = diplomas.");
mc(3,4,"Bourdieu","A family's private library of rare books and paintings is an example of…","Objectified cultural capital",["Embodied cultural capital","Institutionalised cultural capital","Social capital"],"Objectified cultural capital = cultural items such as books, instruments and art.");
mc(2,4,"Bourdieu","A master's degree from an elite university is which form of capital?","Institutionalised cultural capital",["Embodied cultural capital","Social capital","Symbolic capital"],"Institutionalised cultural capital = degrees and diplomas.");
mc(2,4,"Bourdieu","Getting a job interview because your uncle knows the manager is an example of…","Social capital",["Cultural capital","Economic capital","Symbolic capital"],"Social capital = networks and useful relationships.");
mc(3,4,"Bourdieu","For Bourdieu, the fact that someone “naturally” likes opera or jazz and finds other tastes vulgar is explained by…","Habitus shaped by class and upbringing",["Innate individual talent","Random personal preference","Economic capital alone"],"Distinction (1979): “good taste” is socially learned, not purely personal.");
mc(3,4,"Ridgeway","In status construction theory, how can a neutral difference between two groups become an inequality?","Through repeated interactions that tie it to unequal competence and respect",["Through a legal decision that ranks the groups","Through a biological difference in ability","Through the choice of the group itself"],"Everyday interactions turn initially neutral differences into shared status beliefs.");
